      "msg_0ab5243a0658b570016ada162bd4e481959c6f0ef5a0a66bc7": {
        "id": "msg_0ab5243a0658b570016ada162bd4e481959c6f0ef5a0a66bc7",
        "message": {
          "id": "msg_0ab5243a0658b570016ada162bd4e481959c6f0ef5a0a66bc7",
          "author": {
            "role": "assistant",
            "name": null,
            "metadata": {}
          },
          "create_time": 1792677419.853,
          "update_time": 1792677429.436,
          "content": {
            "content_type": "text",
            "parts": [
              "The quantity equal to zero is the **collision-counting integral**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\int_D \\rho(H,H_x)\\bigl(H_{xx}^{\\,2}-H_xH_{xxx}\\bigr)\\,dt\\,dx\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{p\\in D\\\\H(p)=H_x(p)=0}}\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{m_p}{2}\\right\\rfloor .\n}\n\\]\nHere \\(H\\) is the heat-deformed xi function, \\(m_p\\) is the spatial multiplicity at a collision, and \\(\\rho\\) is a smooth, normalized weight concentrated near \\((H,H_x)=(0,0)\\). The identity requires a nonzero boundary jet and support small enough to respect that boundary.\n\nThus the integral counts collisions with positive integer weights: a double or triple event contributes one; a quadruple event contributes two. **An integral of zero means no collisions inside \\(D\\).**\n\nIn our test, the weight \\(\\rho(H,H_x)\\) vanished everywhere: the entire jet stayed outside its tiny support. Neither \\(H\\) nor the derivative expression was shown to vanish. Proving that support avoidance already supplied a direct proof of no collisions, so integration added no strength.\n\n**The formula is potentially useful.** Its integrand is smooth even at collisions, and a rigorous upper bound below one would exclude every collision in the domain. The interesting research question is whether its signed integral can be estimated more effectively than checking nonvanishing throughout the region or computing boundary winding.\n\nThis first test validated the formula’s implementation and accounting. It did **not** establish that more powerful integral estimate or settle the formula’s broader usefulness."
            ]
          },
          "status": "finished_successfully",
          "end_turn": true,
